{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The joint distribution $P(X, Y)$ can be written as $P(Y | X) \\cdot P(X)$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:06:07\u201300:06:41] So this means that the observation of Y in the case when X and Y are interdependent provides us some information that allows us to reduce the variance of X. And again, this is a quite obvious fact, but it's so useful to be able to prove it. Okay, let's go further. So if we return to this definition, we can immediately get the expression for joint distribution P of X, Y that is equal to P of X given y times p of y."
    },
    {
      "claim": "By symmetry, $P(X, Y) = P(X | Y) \\cdot P(Y)$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:06:07\u201300:06:41] ... we can immediately get the expression for joint distribution P of X, Y that is equal to P of X given y times p of y. And of course, due to symmetry, we can also write it down as p of y given x times p of x."
    },
    {
      "claim": "For $n$ variables, the joint distribution $P(X_1, X_2, \\dots, X_n)$ decomposes as $\\prod_{i=1}^{n} P(X_i | X_1, X_2, \\dots, X_{i-1})$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:06:41\u201300:07:23] ... by induction, we can prove the same for arbitrary number of random variables. So you can even see the joint distribution, p of x1, xn. It can always be decomposed as a product of one dimensional conditional distributions."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The forward ordering decomposition is $P(X_1) \\cdot P(X_2 | X_1) \\cdot \\dots \\cdot P(X_n | X_{n-1}, \\dots, X_1)$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:07:23\u201300:07:56] For example, as field X1 given X2 Xn times field X2 given X3 Xn times field Xn minus one given Xn times field Xn."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The reverse ordering decomposition is $P(X_n) \\cdot P(X_{n-1} | X_n) \\cdot \\dots \\cdot P(X_1 | X_2, \\dots, X_n)$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:07:23\u201300:07:56] And again, there's a large number of different decompositions. So for example, we can order the arguments and get another decomposition, say field Xn given X1. Xn minus one, and so on. X2 given X1, p of X1."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The identity is called 'product row' (a typo for 'product rule')",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:07:56\u201300:08:35] And the size decomposition has special name, and it's called product row."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The chain rule is a sequential decomposition of joint distributions",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:07:56\u201300:08:35] And it states that any joint distribution can always be decomposed as a product of one-dimensional conditional distributions, a kind of a chain row."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The product rule and chain rule are the same identity",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:07:56\u201300:08:35] This identity is known as product row. And it states that any joint distribution can always be decomposed as a product of one-dimensional conditional distributions, a kind of a chain row."
    }
  ],
  "clarity_1_to_5": 4,
  "caveat": "The ASR uses 'product row' and 'chain row' which are likely typos for 'product rule' and 'chain rule'. The lecture mentions 'product row' and 'chain row' but the notes correctly identify them as typos."
}
